    <h1 data-split>Still Looking for a Side Project Topic? You Can Stop Searching</h1>
    <p class="lede prose">Side projects matter, but learning notes and worklogs show your problem-solving process, communication skills, and technical growth too.</p>
    <p class="links-line">Originally published in Traditional Chinese &middot; September 4, 2022 &middot; <a href="/writing">All writing</a></p>
    <div class="longform prose" style="margin-top:34px">
      <p class="rv">This article is for engineers with at least a year of experience. If you're switching into tech from another field, I'd still recommend a structured bootcamp: Hexschool [六角學院], AppWorks, AlphaCamp, or one of the Udemy bootcamps.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">My Experience</h2>
      <p class="rv">I was a Computer Science grad who'd worked on app projects in school and held a Java certification, so finding my first job wasn't hard. Demand was high, training engineers took time, and relevant experience usually got you an offer.</p>
      <p class="rv">In my first job as a Java engineer, I found that a lot of my non-CS colleagues had trained at places like the Institute for Information Industry (III) [資策會]. They loved coding, not just the salary. I was struck by junior colleagues who stayed after hours to talk about code and chase technical breakthroughs.</p>
      <p class="rv">"So this is what an engineer's life looks like."</p>
      <p class="rv">"Beyond enjoying writing code, you need genuine curiosity about how things work under the hood."</p>
      <p class="rv">Back then, I hadn't found that joy myself. That came later, when I moved into a Project Manager (PM) role.</p>
      <p class="rv">As a PM, I managed a dream team and realized engineers just build side projects on their own. No matter how small the idea, from a simple chatbot to a full application, they build it themselves. That shift in perspective broadened how I saw things.</p>
      <p class="rv">Over time I formed my own idea of what an engineer is: someone who solves problems and keeps their technical skills current.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">How Are Side Projects Viewed on a Resume?</h2>
      <p class="rv">I'm about five years into my career now, and I've interviewed a lot of engineers. A resume packed with different frameworks and technologies catches the eye. Some candidates lay out the project's purpose and tech stack on GitHub; others just leave a short note on the background.</p>
      <p class="rv">But a side project alone can't tell you:</p>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>How do you troubleshoot?</li><li>Did you write the code yourself, or follow an instructor step by step?</li><li>When you hit a problem, did you solve it yourself or use the answer key?</li><li>Do you understand what you built, and why?</li><li>What does your learning curve look like?</li></ol>
      <p class="rv">Side projects are valuable. They show initiative, ambition, and a track record of building things. But in an interview, they're mostly a conversation starter, a bonus, not the deciding factor.</p>
      <p class="rv">Preparing for an interview is only the first step. An interview can't expose a candidate's real engineering rigor or their actual attitude toward problem-solving. Some candidates sound great in interviews, and then, once hired, turn out to have oversold themselves.</p>
      <p class="rv">That's why a lot of companies run one to two hour take-home tasks or live pair-coding sessions. Watching someone work reveals their technical competence, communication skills, and how they solve problems in real time.</p>
      <p class="rv">A static side project can't convey any of that. Assessing a candidate takes direct interaction. From my experience interviewing people, hiring decisions usually come down to a few factors:</p>
      <ol class="num-list"><li>Professional competence and engineering rigor</li><li>Personal traits: communication, responsiveness, flexibility in solving problems</li><li>Relevant experience: whether their background fits the role</li><li>Interview rapport: whether you understand each other and communicate well</li><li>Compensation expectations: whether the budget and the candidate's expectations line up</li></ol>
      <p class="rv">So should you worry if you don't have a side project? Not necessarily. If you love building things, you'll probably end up making something anyway, a Chrome extension, a CI/CD pipeline built from scratch.</p>
      <p class="rv">But what about people who keep up with tech trends and like learning new tools, without the time or inclination to build a full side project? Is there a faster way to show what you can do? Yes.</p>
      <p class="rv"><a href="https://lucycbiz.com/how-to-face-between-jobs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Related Reading: Overcoming Unemployment Anxiety to Maximize Job Opportunities</a></p>
      <h2 class="rv">How to Quickly Build a Side Project Alternative</h2>
      <p class="rv">Side projects can be big or small. The skills you pick up, and how they help you solve problems, matter more than the project itself.</p>
      <p class="rv">The core idea is simple. If something helps a reviewer understand your thought process, your learning curve, your ideas, it works as a side project, just in a different form.</p>
      <p class="rv">The fastest way to show that is by keeping <strong>learning notes and worklogs</strong>.</p>
      <p class="rv">An engineer's technical notes reflect how they think, how they solve problems, their mindset. Well-structured notes show how you weigh trade-offs and land on better solutions. You don't always need a finished product to prove what you can do. If you're aiming for Google, Apple, Amazon, or another big international company, a solid side project helps show ambition and technical depth. If you don't have one yet, worklogs close that gap.</p>
      <h2 class="rv">How to Maintain Worklogs and Technical Notes</h2>
      <p class="rv">I recommend Hexschool's seven-week "<a href="https://lucycbiz.com/hexschool-notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Note King</a>" note-taking portfolio program.</p>
      <p class="rv">If you keep public technical notes, on HackMD say, and link them on your resume, that communicates your learning curve and your interest in the craft. Notes convey things raw code can't. Naming conventions, architecture, comments, and coding style show technical competence, but they don't show how you communicate. Writing and interviews cover that.</p>
      <p class="rv"><em>Lucy's tech notes</em></p>
      <p class="rv">So if you're still worried about not having a side project, stop hunting for ideas.</p>
      <p class="rv"><strong>Your notes are your portfolio.</strong></p>
      <h2 class="rv">Do Non-Engineers Need Side Projects?</h2>
      <p class="rv">In my view, side projects aren't just a software thing. An architect building a backyard treehouse has a side project. A marketer running a niche Instagram account has one too. Anything you build or pursue because you're into it counts.</p>
      <p class="rv">The value is in what you pick up along the way. If you document and share your insights as you go, that's one of the best ways to present the work.</p>
      <p class="rv">People are drawn in by the final outcome, but they stay to understand how it got built. Document the process, and you'll leave a stronger impression than just handing someone a result with no context.</p>
